 The stronger test adds four conditions. The second implementer (a) has not seen the reference source; (b) reads the specification and confirms by SHA-256 that it is the document the reference cites; (c) implements in a different language, so that runtime defaults cannot be inherited by accident; (d) runs blind against the published inputs and reports per-ring match or mismatch before seeing the reference outputs.
 
-Condition (a) is a statement by the implementer and cannot be proven; it is recorded as such in the anchored record ("implementation_2_source_seen: none of implementation_1 (implementer's statement)"). Condition (b) is checkable and was checked: the specification's SHA-256, 9ccba2e325fd2a555fcdb2dec519b8c6bf7a669064674846aea98ecfff824e3d, matches the value cited at the head of the reference builder.
+Condition (a) is a statement by the implementer and cannot be proven; it is recorded as such in the anchored record ("implementation_2_source_seen: none of implementation_1 (implementer's statement)"). In practice this means: workers/hs-ledger/nenrin/ring-v1/make_ring.py was never opened, fetched, or consulted in any form, before, during, or after the reimplementation; its path and name were learned only from the anchored record afterward. The build started from NENRIN_SPEC_v1.md's Layer 3 section and this paper's own Section 3 description of the fields, the inputs, and the canonicalization rule — nothing else.
+
+Condition (b) is checkable and was checked: the specification's SHA-256, 9ccba2e325fd2a555fcdb2dec519b8c6bf7a669064674846aea98ecfff824e3d, matches the value cited at the head of the reference builder. It was computed by fetching NENRIN_SPEC_v1.md fresh and hashing the response before a line of the reimplementation was written, so that a stale or substituted copy of the specification could not silently pass against the wrong document.
+
+Condition (c) holds structurally, not just nominally: the reference is Python, the reimplementation is Node.js, and the two share no runtime, no standard library, and no default serialization behaviour. This is what exposed the seam reported in Section 6 — a shared runtime could have inherited the reference's key-ordering behaviour without either implementer noticing that the specification had never stated it.
+
+Condition (d) was met by pulling all nine committed history exports and all eight published ring files fresh from github.com/ogasurfproject-jpg/mcp-conduct-register at the moment of the run, not from any copy already on disk, and running the builder's own verify mode, which rebuilds each ring from its history export and compares the result byte for byte against the claimed file, printing the first differing field on any mismatch, before either implementer's account of the other's result was read.
 
