-The reimplementation succeeded, and the reason it did is the most useful thing in this paper: the implementer checked one thing that a port would have assumed.
+The reimplementation succeeded, and the reason it did is the more useful result: one behaviour was checked rather than assumed, and the check is what closed the gap.
 
-Python's json.dumps(sort_keys=True) sorts dictionary keys at every level of nesting. JavaScript's JSON.stringify sorts nothing; it emits keys in insertion order. A port that reproduced the ring-building logic exactly and serialized with JSON.stringify(obj, null, 2) would produce output that looks like a ring, parses as a ring, carries the same counts, and hashes to a different value. Neither implementation alone could detect this: each is self-consistent. Only the comparison of bytes across the two exposes it. The second implementer did not fall into it. He checked the reference behaviour rather than assuming it, and added an explicit recursive key sort (objects sorted, arrays left in order) before serialization. A naive port would have looked plausible and been wrong in a way that neither implementation alone could have caught; that is why the seam, and not the byte match, is the finding.
+Python's `json.dumps(sort_keys=True)` sorts dictionary keys at every level of nesting. JavaScript's `JSON.stringify` sorts nothing; it emits object keys in insertion order. A port that reproduced the ring-building logic exactly and serialized with `JSON.stringify(obj, null, 2)` directly would have produced output that looks like a ring, parses as a ring, carries the same counts, and hashes to a different value on any object whose insertion order differs from sorted order — which, for this schema, is most of them. Neither implementation alone can detect this: each is internally self-consistent, and a single implementer re-running their own code will get their own answer back every time. Only a second, independent implementation exposes it, and only if that implementation is compared byte for byte rather than field for field. The reimplementation adds an explicit recursive key sort — every plain object's keys sorted, every array's own order left untouched — immediately before serialization, applied once at the top of the ring object so it reaches every nested level the way Python's single flag does in one pass. That one function is the entire fix; everything else in the builder can be wrong in some unrelated way and this would not catch it, which is a limit of the check as much as a description of it.
 
-The seam is real and it is the specification's fault, not the implementer's. The specification defines the canonical form by reference to a language runtime ("Python's json.dumps with these options") rather than by stating the rules. It was reproducible in practice; it was under-specified in principle. RFC 8785 exists precisely to remove this class of seam, and a future revision of the specification should either adopt it or state the serialization rules language-neutrally: key ordering by Unicode code point, string escaping, number formatting, whitespace, and the trailing newline.
+The seam belongs to the specification, not to either implementer. Section 3.3 defines the canonical form by naming a language and a function call ("Python's `json.dumps` with these options") rather than by stating the rules the call happens to enforce. That definition was reproducible in practice, because both implementers happened to land on the same effective ruleset, but it was under-specified in principle, because nothing prevented a second implementer from landing on a different, equally reasonable one and never finding out. RFC 8785 exists for exactly this: to state key ordering, string escaping, number formatting, and whitespace independently of any runtime's defaults, so that "canonical" describes the bytes rather than a call site.
 
